# ---- Generate research_report.md --------------------------------------------
report_md = f"""# Failure-Aware SEAL-NFT: Research Report

## 1. Abstract
This report documents a small-scale, reproducible experiment testing whether an
adapted NFT-style objective with failure memory, deterministic failure
classification, a validation-gated update, and rollback ("FASA" / Failure-Aware
NFT) improves sample efficiency, stability, and continual-learning retention over
SEAL-style positive-only RFT, on a Llama-family ~1B policy model doing SQuAD
knowledge-incorporation self-edits.

## 2. Research Question
{"When a small language model repeatedly generates self-edits to incorporate new "
"knowledge, does explicitly learning from its failed adaptations produce better "
"sample efficiency, stability, and continual-learning retention than SEAL-style "
"positive-only RFT?"}

## 3. Hypotheses
- H1: FASA is more sample-efficient than RFT (fewer self-edit evaluations to reach a target accuracy).
- H2: FASA achieves higher final validation/test accuracy than RFT.
- H3: FASA has lower catastrophic forgetting / better backward transfer under continual adaptation.
- H4: FASA is more robust to noisy/sparse reward signals than RFT.

## 4. Experimental Architecture
Passage -> self-edit generation (K sampled candidates) -> per-candidate fresh-LoRA
inner-loop adaptation (candidate isolation enforced) -> deterministic EM/F1
evaluation on validation questions -> reward -> method-specific outer update
(RFT / adapted-NFT / Negative-SFT / FASA) -> optional validation-gated rollback.

## 5. Dataset
SQuAD ({DATASET_NAME}), {len(EXPERIMENT_CONTEXTS)} contexts used, each split into
strict adaptation/validation/test question sets (never overlapping). This
reproduces the core SEAL knowledge-incorporation structure (passage -> self-edit
-> LoRA -> QA recall without the passage) but is NOT a full reproduction of the
original SEAL paper's training pipeline, scale, or hyperparameters.

## 6. Models
- Policy: {POLICY_MODEL if RUN_LLM_EXPERIMENT else '(not loaded -- no GPU)'}
- Verifier: {VERIFIER_MODEL if (RUN_LLM_EXPERIMENT and not VERIFIER_USE_EXTERNAL_API) else ('external API' if VERIFIER_USE_EXTERNAL_API else '(not loaded)')}
- The policy model never evaluates its own outputs; only the deterministic EM/F1
  scorer and the separate frozen verifier are used for evaluation.

## 7. Baselines
Base (no adaptation), RFT (SEAL/ReST-style positive-only), adapted NFT
(positive+negative, standardized/clipped weights -- NOT a verbatim reproduction of
any specific published NFT algorithm), Negative-SFT (simple positive+negative
NLL control).

## 8. Reward Design
Primary reward R = delta validation EM (alpha=1, beta=0, gamma=0). Composite
reward (target/generalization/forgetting terms) implemented but disabled by
default; only used in explicit ablations.

## 9. NFT Adaptation (honesty note)
The "NFT" objective implemented here standardizes rewards across K candidates,
clips the resulting weights to [-{20}, {20}], and applies a signed-NLL update
(negative weight => discourage). This is an adaptation to the structured
self-edit setting, not a literal reproduction of the original NFT formulation.

## 10. Failure Memory
FIFO buffer, capacity {MEMORY_SIZE}, storing every candidate with reward <= 0
along with a deterministic rule-based failure_type classification.

## 11. Rollback
Validation-gated: an update is rejected if validation EM drops by more than
{ROLLBACK_THRESHOLD}. Test performance is never used for this decision.

## 12. Evaluation Protocol
Deterministic normalized EM / token-F1 is the primary metric wherever ground
truth exists. The independent verifier is used only for semantic/error analysis
and reward-hacking checks, and was validated against ground truth before any
use (Section 16 results below).

